From: Erfan Abdi <erfangplus@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] apklayer: the guest's AT_HWCAP and /proc/cpuinfo advertise the same ARM64 features

Two hand-written tables describe the guest CPU's features: guest_loader_arch.cc's AT_HWCAP (patch 0005 serves it as the guest's
/proc/self/auxv; getauxval reports it) and FormatGuestCpuinfo's "Features" line. They had drifted apart - AT_HWCAP carried 8 bits while
/proc/cpuinfo listed 16 - so /proc/cpuinfo advertised evtstrm, fphp, asimdhp, cpuid, asimdrdm, lrcpc, dcpop and asimddp that getauxval
denied. On a real device the kernel derives both from the same CPU capabilities and they always agree; the mismatch is what code that
cross-checks the two (anti-tamper, fingerprinting) reads as a faked CPU. Make them equal on the 14 features berberis actually provides:
AT_HWCAP gains FP16, RDM, LRCPC, DC CVAP and dotprod; the Features line drops evtstrm (the generic-timer event stream is not provided)
and cpuid. cpuid (HWCAP_CPUID) is left out of both on purpose: the decoder emulates MIDR_EL1/CTR_EL0 but not the ID_AA64* registers, and
code that sees the bit reads them directly (mrs x9, ID_AA64PFR1_EL1), which the interpreter raises SIGILL on. Emulating the ID_AA64*
reads (a real kernel traps them) is the follow-up that would let the bit be set. AT_HWCAP2 stays empty. Module libberberis_arm64.

diff --git a/guest_loader/arm64/guest_loader_arch.cc b/guest_loader/arm64/guest_loader_arch.cc
index 9d2eddd..e5adb50 100644
--- a/guest_loader/arm64/guest_loader_arch.cc
+++ b/guest_loader/arm64/guest_loader_arch.cc
@@ -52,7 +52,22 @@ GuestAddr InitKernelArgs(GuestAddr guest_sp,
       (1UL << 5) |   // HWCAP_SHA1
       (1UL << 6) |   // HWCAP_SHA2
       (1UL << 7) |   // HWCAP_CRC32
-      (1UL << 8);    // HWCAP_ATOMICS (LSE)
+      (1UL << 8) |   // HWCAP_ATOMICS (LSE)
+      // apklayer patch: the features the interpreter also provides and the synthesized /proc/cpuinfo Features line lists. getauxval
+      // (this auxv, patch 0005 serves it as /proc/self/auxv) and /proc/cpuinfo both describe the guest CPU, and on a real device the
+      // kernel derives them from the same capabilities; they disagreed here (cpuinfo advertised 8 features the HWCAP did not), which
+      // code that cross-checks the two reads as a tampered CPU. Only features berberis translates are added (FP16, RDM, LRCPC, DC CVAP,
+      // dotprod, CPUID register access); evtstrm, which is not provided, is dropped from the Features line instead (open_emulation.cc).
+      (1UL << 9) |   // HWCAP_FPHP (half-precision FP)
+      (1UL << 10) |  // HWCAP_ASIMDHP (half-precision ASIMD)
+      // HWCAP_CPUID (bit 11) is intentionally NOT set: the decoder emulates MIDR_EL1/CTR_EL0 but not the ID_AA64* registers, and code
+      // that sees HWCAP_CPUID reads them directly (MRS x9, ID_AA64PFR1_EL1 = 0xd5380429) - which the decoder rejects as undefined and
+      // the interpreter raises SIGILL on (PGR died 1.5 s in). A real kernel traps and emulates those EL0 reads; until the decoder does
+      // too, do not advertise the bit. The Features line omits "cpuid" to match (open_emulation.cc).
+      (1UL << 12) |  // HWCAP_ASIMDRDM (RDMA)
+      (1UL << 15) |  // HWCAP_LRCPC (RCpc load-acquire)
+      (1UL << 16) |  // HWCAP_DCPOP (DC CVAP)
+      (1UL << 20);   // HWCAP_ASIMDDP (dotprod)
 
   // Bionic linker_main passes AT_EXECFN's string to __libc_shared_globals's
   // init_progname; the riscv64 path also doesn't set it, but providing the
diff --git a/kernel_api/arm64/open_emulation.cc b/kernel_api/arm64/open_emulation.cc
index 7731242..367c208 100644
--- a/kernel_api/arm64/open_emulation.cc
+++ b/kernel_api/arm64/open_emulation.cc
@@ -129,8 +129,10 @@ std::string FormatGuestCpuinfo(int num_cpus) {
     int n = snprintf(block, sizeof(block),
                      "processor\t: %d\n"
                      "BogoMIPS\t: 38.40\n"
-                     "Features\t: fp asimd evtstrm aes pmull sha1 sha2 crc32 "
-                     "atomics fphp asimdhp cpuid asimdrdm lrcpc dcpop asimddp\n"
+                     // apklayer patch: no evtstrm (the generic-timer event stream is not provided); the list now matches AT_HWCAP
+                     // (guest_loader_arch.cc) bit for bit, so code that cross-checks /proc/cpuinfo against getauxval agrees.
+                     "Features\t: fp asimd aes pmull sha1 sha2 crc32 "
+                     "atomics fphp asimdhp asimdrdm lrcpc dcpop asimddp\n"
                      "CPU implementer\t: 0x41\n"
                      "CPU architecture: 8\n"
                      "CPU variant\t: 0x1\n"
